# ===========================================================================================
# .gitignore — github.com/johniliad/johniliad
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This repository is a PUBLIC GitHub Pages site and the public profile README at the same time.
# Anything committed here is world-readable the moment it is pushed, is mirrored by the Pages
# CDN, is copied by every scraper that already visits the domain, and survives in the git
# history even after it is deleted in a later commit. Treat "git commit" as "publish".
#
# The rule this file enforces: a path may be committed only if it is part of the deployed
# identity bridge — the nine entries below and the images in assets/img/ — and nothing else.
#
#   .gitignore  .nojekyll  CNAME  README.md  DEPLOY.md  index.html  robots.txt  sitemap.xml
#   .well-known/security.txt
#   assets/img/*
#
# Everything else in this folder is working material: scratch scripts, screenshots, crop and
# validator helpers, DNS and TLS dumps, crawl exports, browser network captures, editor state,
# cloud-sync copies, credentials.
#
# Check before every push:
#   git ls-files                                              # what will be published
#   git status --short --ignored                              # what is withheld, and why
#   git ls-files | Where-Object { git check-ignore -q --no-index -- $_; $LASTEXITCODE -eq 0 }
#                                                             # must print nothing: no tracked path is ignored
# Do not use "git ls-files | git check-ignore --no-index --stdin -v" for that third check: PowerShell
# appends a carriage return to every piped line, so --stdin receives ".gitignore\r", which no rule
# matches, and -v additionally prints negation matches. Its output cannot separate a real hit from
# that noise in either direction.
#
# Syntax reminders: "#" starts a comment only on a line of its own (a trailing "#" is pattern
# text); a leading "/" anchors the pattern to the repository root; a trailing "/" matches
# directories only; "**" spans any depth; and "!" cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is already excluded — which is why section 13 re-includes directories first.
# ===========================================================================================

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The deployed set is never ignored
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No rule below matches these paths; the negations are insurance. They exist so that a future
# broad rule ("*", ".*", "*.md", "*.json", "/assets") cannot quietly remove something the live
# site depends on. Dropping .nojekyll, for instance, would make Pages run Jekyll and discard
# every file whose name begins with an underscore; dropping CNAME would detach the subdomain.
!.gitignore
!.nojekyll
!CNAME
!README.md
!DEPLOY.md
!index.html
!robots.txt
!sitemap.xml
!/assets/img/**
# The RFC 9116 disclosure contact is part of the deployed set too. Its directory is re-included
# before the file, because "!" cannot re-add a file whose parent directory is already excluded —
# section 13's "/*" would otherwise hide the whole directory.
!/.well-known/
!/.well-known/security.txt

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Local scratch and session artefacts
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Working material for building and checking the bridge: headless-browser captures, crop and
# screenshot helpers, validation and link-check reports, saved copies of the live profile HTML,
# DNS and certificate dumps. None of it is served; all of it describes how the site was made.
_tmp/
_tmp.*
_scratch/
_work/
_local/
sandbox/
tmp/
temp/

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Stray files at the repository root
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every legitimate image lives under assets/img/, and every legitimate document is one of the
# nine names in section 1. A raster image, an archive, a capture, a spreadsheet or a script
# sitting at the root is therefore a leftover — a crop, a download, an export, a one-off helper
# — and must not be published. This failure mode has already happened twice in this repository.
/*.png
/*.jpg
/*.jpeg
/*.webp
/*.gif
/*.bmp
/*.tif
/*.tiff
/*.pdf
/*.csv
/*.har
/*.pcap
/*.pcapng
/*.mp4
/*.mov
/*.webm
/*.exe
/*.msi
/*.dll
/*.scr
/*.ps1
/*.psm1
/*.bat
/*.cmd
/*.sh
/*.tar
/*.tar.gz
/*.tgz
/*.zip
/*.rar
/*.7z
# These are root-scoped on purpose: assets/img/ keeps its .webp/.jpg/.png files.
# /favicon.ico, /site.webmanifest and /manifest.json are deliberately absent from this list —
# those would be legitimate deployments if ever added.
# If a script is ever genuinely wanted at the root, commit it deliberately: git add -f deploy.ps1
# Duplicate copies of a real page or document, produced by Explorer ("index (1).html") or by
# OneDrive on a second machine ("index-DESKTOP-8F2A.html"). These are the most dangerous kind of
# stray file, because they look exactly like the deployed document and carry stale content.
/index-*.html
/README-*.md
/DEPLOY-*.md
/*-Copy.*
/* (*)*

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Credentials, keys and tokens — the section that matters most in a public repository
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No credential belongs to this project, but a single accidental copy would be world-readable
# immediately and would remain readable in the git history after any later deletion. This block
# is deliberately over-broad: a false positive costs one "git add -f", a false negative cannot
# be undone. Rotating to prove the point is not how you want to spend the afternoon.
.env
.env.*
.envrc
*.env
# Private keys and cryptographic stores
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
*.jks
*.keystore
*.ppk
*.kdbx
*.kdb
*.ovpn
# SSH and GNU Privacy Guard material
id_rsa*
id_dsa*
id_ecdsa*
id_ed25519*
.ssh/
.gnupg/
# Cloud, registry and package-manager credentials
.aws/
.azure/
.netrc
_netrc
.git-credentials
.npmrc
.pypirc
.yarnrc
# Rendered configuration, service-account files, scribbled secrets
credentials*.json
secrets*.json
secret*.json
service-account*.json
api-keys*
api_keys*
token.txt
tokens.txt
passwords*
*.secret
*.token

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Reconnaissance and verification output
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DNS, WHOIS, certificate and crawl material produced while checking the domain and the live
# Pages surface, plus browser network captures. HAR files record request and response headers
# including cookies and bearer tokens; packet captures can contain anything that crossed the
# wire, which on this machine means more than this repository.
*.har
*.pcap
*.pcapng
*.etl
*.evtx
dns*.txt
*dns*.csv
zone*.txt
*zone*.csv
dig*.txt
nslookup*.txt
whois*.txt
*.zone
lighthouse-report*
*rich-results*.json
*.axe.json
axe-*.json
pa11y*.json
/report*.json
/*-report.json
/validation-report*

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Operating-system noise
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Explorer thumbnails and shortcuts leak local paths and account names; crash and event dumps
# can carry memory contents. None of it is ever part of a published page.
# Windows
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
ehthumbs_vista.db
Desktop.ini
desktop.ini
*.lnk
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.stackdump
*.dmp
*.dump
# macOS
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100/
.Trashes/
.fseventsd/
.VolumeIcon.icns
.com.apple.timemachine.donotpresent
# Linux and generic
.directory
.nfs*
*~
~$*

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Editors, IDEs and language-server state
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor scratch holds unsaved buffers, local history and language-server caches; the local
# history folder in particular keeps full copies of files that were edited and reverted.
.vscode/
.vscode-test/
.idea/
.vs/
*.iml
*.suo
*.user
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates
*.code-workspace
.history/
*.swp
*.swo
*.swn
*.sublime-workspace
.project
.classpath
.settings/
.eslintcache
.stylelintcache

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. AI assistant and agent scratch
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session transcripts, prompt logs and tool-call dumps are the richest accidental source of text
# that was never meant to be published — including drafts that were cut from the page on purpose.
.aider*
.claude/
.continue/
.cursor/
.codeium/
.copilot/
.llm/
prompts/

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. Tooling and build artefacts
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The bridge is static HTML with no build step, so anything this section matches was produced by
# an ad-hoc experiment rather than by the site. The Jekyll entries below are a leftover of this
# repository's first life as a Jekyll Pages site; .nojekyll disables that path now.
node_modules/
.npm/
.yarn/
.pnpm-store/
bower_components/
dist/
build/
out/
coverage/
.nyc_output/
.cache/
.parcel-cache/
.next/
.nuxt/
.svelte-kit/
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.tox/
.sass-cache/
.jekyll-cache/
.jekyll-metadata
_site/
.bundle/
vendor/bundle/

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. Logs and traces
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build, crawl and console output. Console logs from a headless run routinely capture full URLs,
# cookies and error payloads; nothing here is going to be served by GitHub Pages.
*.log
logs/
*.trace

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. Media, design sources and office documents
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files a person might drop in while producing the page, none of which belong on a public site:
# layered design sources, video recordings, raw camera files and Office exports. Note that
# .webp, .jpg and .png are NOT listed here — the deployed set needs them under assets/img/.
*.psd
*.xcf
*.sketch
*.fig
*.indd
*.ai
*.mkv
*.avi
*.webm
*.mp4
*.mov
*.raw
*.cr2
*.nef
*.doc
*.docx
*.xls
*.xlsx
*.ppt
*.pptx
*.odt
*.ods
*.odp
*.pages
*.numbers
*.keynote

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12. Cloud-sync and copy artefacts
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This folder lives inside OneDrive, which manufactures per-machine duplicates and conflict
# copies whenever the same file is touched on two devices. Duplicates of the site's own documents
# ("index-DESKTOP-8F2A.html") are caught by the root guards in section 3; sync debris and conflict
# markers are caught here. These files look like real pages, they are stale, and they must never
# be published next to the real ones.
# Case is spelled out three ways on purpose: core.ignorecase makes Windows match case-insensitively
# while a Linux CI checkout does not, and a rule that only works on one of them fails silently on
# the other.
*conflict*
*Conflict*
*CONFLICT*
*.part
*.partial
*.crdownload
*.download

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 13. Optional strict mode — deny everything that is not explicitly deployed
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The block below is inert. Uncomment it to invert the logic of the whole file: a path becomes
# eligible for commit only if it is named here, so every new image must be added both to this
# list and to DEPLOY.md §1 before it can deploy at all. Choose it only if you would rather see a
# visible 404 than risk publishing something stray — the allow-list is maintained by hand, so it
# can lag behind the assets directory. Directories are re-included before their contents, which
# is required for "!" to work at all.
#
# /*
# !/.gitignore
# !/.nojekyll
# !/CNAME
# !/README.md
# !/DEPLOY.md
# !/index.html
# !/robots.txt
# !/sitemap.xml
# !/.well-known/
# !/.well-known/security.txt
# !/assets/
# /assets/*
# !/assets/img/
# /assets/img/*
# !/assets/img/*.webp
# !/assets/img/*.png
# !/assets/img/*.jpg
# !/assets/img/*.svg
# ===========================================================================================
# End of file. If you add a rule, add the reasoning above it: a silent ignore is how a needed
# asset disappears from a deploy six months from now.
# ===========================================================================================
